# .dockerignore - controls what enters the BUILD CONTEXT.
#
# Docker does NOT read .gitignore. Without this file, `docker build` tars up the
# entire directory (including node_modules and .git) and ships it to the daemon
# before the first instruction even runs.
#
# Three reasons this matters:
#   1. Speed      - a 200MB context is uploaded on every single build.
#   2. Caching    - any change to any non-ignored file busts the COPY cache.
#   3. SECURITY   - a `COPY . .` bakes whatever it finds into a layer. Deleting
#                   it in a later layer does NOT remove it; it stays readable
#                   via `docker history` / `docker save`.

# ---- Version control --------------------------------------------------------
# .git holds the FULL history. If a secret was ever committed and later removed,
# it is still in here and would ship inside the image.
.git
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.github

# ---- Dependencies -----------------------------------------------------------
# Reinstalled inside the image by `npm ci`. Host node_modules is built for
# Windows; copying it in would ship native binaries that cannot run on Alpine.
node_modules
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# ---- Tests & coverage -------------------------------------------------------
# Coverage output is generated, never an input to a build.
coverage

# NOTE: *.test.js / *.spec.js / jest.config.* are deliberately NOT listed here.
#
# It is tempting to exclude them - the runtime image has no business carrying
# test code - but .dockerignore applies to the BUILD CONTEXT, which is shared by
# every stage. The `test` stage does `COPY . .` and then `RUN npm test`, so
# excluding the spec files here means Jest finds zero tests and exits 1:
#
#     No tests found, exiting with code 1
#
# ...which fails `docker build --target test` on every single commit.
#
# Test code is kept out of the SHIPPED image by a stronger mechanism: the
# runtime stage copies an explicit allow-list (`package.json`, `server.js`)
# rather than `COPY . .`. Nothing can leak in by accident, because nothing gets
# in unless it is named. See the comment in Dockerfile's runtime stage.

# ---- Docker & CI meta -------------------------------------------------------
# Editing the Dockerfile should not invalidate the cached COPY of the source.
Dockerfile
Dockerfile.*
.dockerignore
docker-compose*.yml
scripts

# ---- Docs -------------------------------------------------------------------
*.md
docs
LICENSE

# ---- Secrets - must never enter the build context --------------------------
.env
.env.*
*.pem
*.key
*.p12
*.pfx
config/*credentials*

# ---- Editor / OS ------------------------------------------------------------
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini

# ---- Scanner output ---------------------------------------------------------
*.sarif
trivy-results.*
gitleaks-report.json

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DELIBERATELY NOT IGNORED:
#   package.json, package-lock.json  -> `npm ci` requires BOTH in the context.
#   server.js                        -> the application itself.
#   server.test.js                   -> the `test` stage runs it. It is excluded
#                                       from the runtime image by explicit COPY,
#                                       not by this file.
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
